  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM EDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nFiltered sunshine this afternoon now that morning stratus has mixed \nout; more humid as well with dew points in the low-70s. All eyes on \nthe upper Great Lakes this afternoon where thunderstorms are \nunderway in extreme northwest Minnesota. As to be expected, CAMs \nthis morning have varied wildly run-to-run regarding the evolution \nof upstream storms these next 24 hours. The general concensus \nis that embedded mid-and-upper level disturbances passing \nthrough energetic northwesterly flow will spark afternoon and \nevening thunderstorms over the upper Midwest today. Storms then spill\nSSE atop a strong ridge in the lee of the Rockies. This track \ngenerally keeps the most robust thunderstorms over Wisconsin and\nIllinois tonight, but there are intermittent runs that send a \ndecaying complex of storms through west-Central Michigan. \n\nWith fading instability tonight, waning bulk shear values, lagging \nupper-level support, and a weakening rise/fall couplet on pressure-\nchange plots, there is medium-high confidence that upstream activity \nweakens as it perhaps limps into to the western- third of the \nforecast area after midnight. Alternatively, it could be dry all\nnight given the best forcing is well north. Also uncertain is \nwhether or not a second complex of storms can quickly develop \ntonight over WI, drifting in locally after sunrise Monday. Some\nCAMs have these two complexes comically close to one other, \nraising significant questions about how quickly the atmosphere \ncould recover. We'll retain some POPs Monday morning to account \nfor this possibility, but confidence is low for severe storms\ntonight through sunrise Monday.\n\nMonday afternoon and Monday night is our best opportunity for \nsevere thunderstorms capable of producing damaging wind gusts. \nAn approaching 50-60 knot 500-mb jet dives in by late afternoon,\nlikely sparking storms over southern WI or central MI which \norganize and drop south through our area. Severe weather \nparameters remain favorable, including enhanced areas of SRH, \nintroducing a chance for embedded tornadoes. Any morning \nactivity will directly impact how the afternoon evolves, but the\ncurrent timing of 2pm to 2am Monday appears on track. Hot on \nMonday as well with highs near 90, especially in the western \nhalf of northern IN. Afternoon heat indicies will approach 100 \ndegrees, but the possibility of cloud debris and thunderstorms \nlimits confidence in any heat- related headlines. \n\nNot as hot the rest of the week as troughing and surface high \npressure settle over the eastern Great Lakes. High pressure \nsoftens toward next weekend, introducing the chance for showers \nand thunderstorms.",
